  "worked_solution": "The dimension of the null space of a matrix is the number of non-pivot columns in the matrix. \n\nAlso, all of the given matrices are in row echelon form.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the statements.\n\n- The matrix $A$ has $3$ pivot columns and no non-pivot column. So, \n\n$$\n\\text{dim(Null}(A))=0.\n$$\n\n- The matrix $B$ has $1$ pivot column and $1$ non-pivot column (namely, the $2$nd column). So, \n\n$$\n\\text{dim(Null}(B))=1.\n$$\n\n- The matrix $C$ has $3$ pivot columns and $1$ non-pivot column (namely, the $4$th column). So, \n\n$$\n\\text{dim(Null}(C))=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\text{dim(Null}(A))+\\text{dim(Null}(B))+\\text{dim(Null}(C))=0+1+1=2.\n$$",
